The display is in **Crystal Palace Park, Bromley, south London**, in the Tidal Lakes’ prehistoric-animal area—on the far side of the main dinosaur island, near the pterosaurs. It is partly submerged and is not easily visible from the lakeside path.

The animal is **_Mosasaurus hoffmanni_**, commonly called a **mosasaur**. It was not technically a dinosaur, but a giant marine lizard that lived roughly **70–66 million years ago**.

When Benjamin Waterhouse Hawkins made the sculpture in 1852–54, only a **Mosasaurus skull** was known, so he built just the head, neck, part of the back and one flipper, leaving the rest underwater.

Later discoveries showed that the missing body was broadly:

- long and streamlined, somewhat like a giant aquatic monitor lizard;
- equipped with **four paddle-like flippers**;
- covered in small scales;
- ending in a powerful, vertically flattened **shark-like tail with a tail fin**.

Modern estimates suggest _Mosasaurus hoffmanni_ reached around **17 metres (56 feet)**, although the Crystal Palace statue itself does not represent the complete animal.
